WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.750
Aesop’s Fables. Part 5.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.701
The Boys and the Frogs.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:18.452
Some mischievous Boys were playing on the edge of a pond, and, catching sight of some Frogs swimming about in the shallow water, they began to amuse themselves by pelting them with stones, and they killed several of them.

00:00:18.452 --> 00:00:24.988
At last one of the Frogs put his head out of the water and said, "Oh, stop! stop!

00:00:24.988 --> 00:00:29.305
I beg of you: what is sport to you is death to us."

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:31.319
The North Wind and the Sun.

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:37.819
A dispute arose between the North Wind and the Sun, each claiming that he was stronger than the other.

00:00:37.819 --> 00:00:45.011
At last they agreed to try their powers upon a traveller, to see which could soonest strip him of his cloak.

00:00:45.011 --> 00:01:03.818
The North Wind had the first try; and, gathering up all his force for the attack, he came whirling furiously down upon the man, and caught up his cloak as though he would wrest it from him by one single effort: but the harder he blew, the more closely the man wrapped it round himself.

00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:05.847
Then came the turn of the Sun.

00:01:05.847 --> 00:01:25.939
At first he beamed gently upon the traveller, who soon unclasped his cloak and walked on with it hanging loosely about his shoulders: then he shone forth in his full strength, and the man, before he had gone many steps, was glad to throw his cloak right off and complete his journey more lightly clad.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:28.349
Persuasion is better than force

00:01:28.349 --> 00:01:30.579
The Mistress and her Servants.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:37.498
A Widow, thrifty and industrious, had two servants, whom she kept pretty hard at work.

00:01:37.498 --> 00:01:44.975
They were not allowed to lie long abed in the mornings, but the old lady had them up and doing as soon as the cock crew.

00:01:44.975 --> 00:01:57.783
They disliked intensely having to get up at such an hour, especially in winter-time: and they thought that if it were not for the cock waking up their Mistress so horribly early, they could sleep longer.

00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.313
So they caught it and wrung its neck.

00:02:00.313 --> 00:02:03.292
But they weren't prepared for the consequences.

00:02:03.292 --> 00:02:12.914
For what happened was that their Mistress, not hearing the cock crow as usual, waked them up earlier than ever, and set them to work in the middle of the night.

00:02:12.914 --> 00:02:14.730
The Goods and the Ills.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:27.337
There was a time in the youth of the world when Goods and Ills entered equally into the concerns of men, so that the Goods did not prevail to make them altogether blessed, nor the Ills to make them wholly miserable.

00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:40.564
But owing to the foolishness of mankind the Ills multiplied greatly in number and increased in strength, until it seemed as though they would deprive the Goods of all share in human affairs, and banish them from the earth.

00:02:40.564 --> 00:02:55.692
The latter, therefore, betook themselves to heaven and complained to Jupiter of the treatment they had received, at the same time praying him to grant them protection from the Ills, and to advise them concerning the manner of their intercourse with men.

00:02:55.692 --> 00:03:11.953
Jupiter granted their request for protection, and decreed that for the future they should not go among men openly in a body, and so be liable to attack from the hostile Ills, but singly and unobserved, and at infrequent and unexpected intervals.

00:03:11.953 --> 00:03:27.279
Hence it is that the earth is full of Ills, for they come and go as they please and are never far away; while Goods, alas! come one by one only, and have to travel all the way from heaven, so that they are very seldom seen.

00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:29.240
The Hares and the Frogs.

00:03:29.855 --> 00:03:40.477
The Hares once gathered together and lamented the unhappiness of their lot, exposed as they were to dangers on all sides and lacking the strength and the courage to hold their own.

00:03:40.477 --> 00:03:54.630
Men, dogs, birds and beasts of prey were all their enemies, and killed and devoured them daily: and sooner than endure such persecution any longer, they one and all determined to end their miserable lives.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:02.004
Thus resolved and desperate, they rushed in a body towards a neighbouring pool, intending to drown themselves.

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:12.735
On the bank were sitting a number of Frogs, who, when they heard the noise of the Hares as they ran, with one accord leaped into the water and hid themselves in the depths.

00:04:12.735 --> 00:04:32.401
Then one of the older Hares who was wiser than the rest cried out to his companions, "Stop, my friends, take heart; don't let us destroy ourselves after all: see, here are creatures who are afraid of us, and who must, therefore, be still more timid than ourselves."
